//! Syscall numbers for the architectures Zygo filters on.
//!
//! Generated by compiling against each architecture's own `<sys/syscall.h>`
//! rather than typed by hand: a wrong number in an allowlist is either a hole
//! in the sandbox or a package that mysteriously stops working, and neither
//! failure is visible by reading the code. Compiling rather than preprocessing
//! also resolves the `__NR3264_*` aliases arm64 uses.
//!
//! Regenerate with `make syscall-tables`.
//!
//! The table covers every name any profile mentions — including the ones that
//! are always *denied*. A number is needed to filter on a syscall at all, so a
//! table built only from the allowlist leaves `permissive` unable to grant
//! `unshare` and makes the denial tests vacuous rather than failing loudly.
//!
//! Names absent from an architecture are absent from its table: arm64 has no
//! `open`, `stat` or `select` at all, only the `*at` and `*6` forms. Asking for
//! one there is not an error — there is simply no number to filter on.

/// `AUDIT_ARCH_*`, the value seccomp compares `seccomp_data.arch` against.
///
/// Checking it is not optional. On a kernel that can run both 64- and 32-bit
/// binaries the same syscall *number* means different things per ABI, so a
/// filter that skips this check is bypassed by re-executing under the other one.
#[cfg(target_arch = "aarch64")]
pub const AUDIT_ARCH: u32 = 0xc000_00b7;
#[cfg(target_arch = "x86_64")]
pub const AUDIT_ARCH: u32 = 0xc000_003e;
#[cfg(not(any(target_arch = "aarch64", target_arch = "x86_64")))]
pub const AUDIT_ARCH: u32 = 0;

